1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,060
Figure seven. Many models.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:28,612
Which model should do which job? Strong models are slow and costly on grind work, and fast models are careless on judgment calls. In a workflow you don't have to pick one. Each node pins its own model by class, fast, balanced, or deep, and Keelson resolves that class to a real model from your provider's catalog.

3
00:00:30,612 --> 00:00:37,328
Here a fast model maps the repo, a deep one plans a change, and a balanced one reviews the plan.

4
00:00:40,012 --> 00:00:43,312
Three phases, three models, one run.

5
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:54,688
The run records which model really answered each node. Haiku explored, Opus planned, and Sonnet reviewed, and called the plan sound.

6
00:00:55,740 --> 00:01:14,488
They can be swapped because no node reads another's conversation. Each one writes its conclusions to a markdown file in the run's folder, and the next node opens that file with a fresh context. The file is the contract, so any phase can change models without the others noticing.

7
00:01:18,288 --> 00:01:24,284
So try a different reviewer. One line: replace balanced with a model from another vendor.

8
00:01:26,388 --> 00:01:28,208
And run it again.

9
00:01:29,713 --> 00:01:39,613
Same graph, same handoff. This reviewer, GPT-6 Luna, reads it differently, calls the plan unsound, and names the step it would fix.

10
00:01:40,513 --> 00:01:51,693
That's the point. Stop arguing about which model is best for a job, and run the experiment. The pins live in the file, the run records what really answered, and a swap is one line.

11
00:01:52,293 --> 00:01:54,377
Right model, right phase.

12
00:01:57,077 --> 00:02:00,769
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.

